[Event "Scotch: 4...Nf6 (Mieses)"]

{The Scotch opens the centre at once with 3.d4. White gets free development and a space edge; Black's best try is quick counterplay against e4.}
1. e4 {Grabs the centre. The pawn on e4 controls d5.}
1... e5 {Classical. The pawn on e5 controls d4.}
2. Nf3 {[%cal Gf3e5] Attacks e5. The knight on f3 eyes the pawn on e5 and controls d4.}
2... Nc6 {Defends. The knight on c6 controls d4.}
3. d4 {[%cal Gd4e5] The Scotch: White challenges e5 straight away instead of the slow Ruy squeeze.}
3... exd4 {Practically forced; ...d6 would be passive.}
4. Nxd4 {[%csl Gd4] The knight recaptures centrally. White has a free hand in the centre.}
4... Nf6 {[%cal Gf6e4] Counterattacks e4 at once. The knight on f6 eyes the pawn on e4 and controls d5.}
 (4... Nxd4 {Trades knights, but it centralises White's queen with tempo-free development.}
5. Qxd4 {[%csl Gd4] A dominant central queen; Black has no quick way to harass it.}
5... Ne7 {Heads for c6 or g6. The knight on e7 controls d5.}
6. Bc4 {[%cal Gc4f7] Develops with an eye on f7. The bishop on c4 eyes the pawn on f7 and controls d5.}
)
5. Nxc6 {Trades to break Black's pawn structure before playing e5.}
5... bxc6 {Recaptures towards the centre. Black gets the b-file but doubled c-pawns.}
6. e5 {[%cal Ge5f6] Kicks the knight away from its best square.}
6... Qe7 {Pins the e5 pawn to the king's file so it can be attacked.}
7. Qe2 {Defends e5 and breaks the pin. The queen on e2 controls e4.}
7... Nd5 {The knight takes a central post.}
8. c4 {[%cal Gc4d5] Kicks the knight again, gaining space. The pawn on c4 eyes the knight on d5.}
8... Ba6 {Pins the c4 pawn against the queen. The bishop on a6 eyes the pawn on c4.}
 (8... Nb6 {Retreats, keeping the c4 pawn under watch.}
9. Nc3 {Simple development; White keeps the e5 space advantage.}
)
9. b3 {Solidly defends c4. White keeps the e5 wedge and a structural edge.} *

[Event "Scotch: 4...Bc5"]

{The classical bishop sortie hits d4. White can either take on c6 or support d4 with Be3/c3.}
1. e4 {Centre. The pawn on e4 controls d5.}
1... e5 {Classical. The pawn on e5 controls d4.}
2. Nf3 {Attacks e5. The knight on f3 eyes the pawn on e5 and controls d4.}
2... Nc6 {Defends. The knight on c6 controls d4.}
3. d4 {The Scotch. The pawn on d4 eyes the pawn on e5.}
3... exd4 {Takes.}
4. Nxd4 {Recaptures. The knight on d4 eyes the knight on c6.}
4... Bc5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Attacks the d4 knight right away. The bishop on c5 eyes the knight on d4.}
5. Nxc6 {Trades off before Black adds pressure. The knight on c6 eyes the queen on d8 and the pawn on a7 and controls d4 and e5.}
 (5. Be3 {Holds d4 instead.}
5... Qf6 {More pressure on d4. The queen on f6 eyes the knight on d4 and the pawn on f2 and controls e5.}
6. c3 {[%csl Gd4] Solidly supports the knight.}
6... Nge7 {Develops; ...Nxd4 ideas remain. The knight on e7 controls d5.}
7. Bc4 {[%cal Gc4f7] Develops actively towards f7. The bishop on c4 eyes the pawn on f7 and controls d5.}
)
5... Qf6 {[%cal Gf6f2] An intermezzo threatening mate on f2. The queen on f6 eyes the knight on c6 and the pawn on b2 and controls d4 and e5.}
6. Qd2 {Covers f2 and keeps the queen flexible. The queen on d2 eyes the pawn on d7 and controls d4 and d5.}
6... dxc6 {Recaptures towards the centre with an open d-file.}
7. Nc3 {Develops and prepares Na4 to hit the c5-bishop.}
7... Be6 {Develops and covers c4. The bishop on e6 eyes the pawn on a2 and controls d5.}
8. Na4 {[%cal Ga4c5] Hunts the bishop pair. The knight on a4 eyes the bishop on c5.} *
